\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{When a marginal precision is a restriction}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{Q>0} be self-adjoint on a finite-dimensional real Euclidean
space #{H} and let #{P} be an orthogonal projector. Assume its image
#{X} and kernel #{Y} are nonzero. In #{H=X\oplus Y}, write
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}A&K\\K^*&D\end{pmatrix},\qquad D>0.}
The [selected precision](fdip-005B) obeys
##{S_P=A-KD^{-1}K^*\preceq A.}
Here #{A=PQ|_X} is the compression, and #{T\preceq A} means that
#{\langle x,(A-T)x\rangle\ge0} for every #{x\in X}.
The following conditions are equivalent:
##{\begin{aligned}
S_P=A&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad K=0\\
&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad QX\subseteq X
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad PQ=QP.
\end{aligned}}
In this case #{A=Q|_X}; call the selector \newvocab{restricting}.
Otherwise it \newvocab{induces a different precision} through
elimination of the complement.}

\proof{
\p{The Schur formula is proved in \ref{fdip-005B}. Its difference
from the compression satisfies
##{\langle x,(A-S_P)x\rangle
 =\|D^{-1/2}K^*x\|^2\ge0.}
It vanishes for all #{x} exactly when #{K^*=0}, since
#{D^{-1/2}} is invertible. The #{Y}-component of #{Q(x,0)} is
#{K^*x}, so the same condition is #{QX\subseteq X}.
Self-adjointness then makes #{Y=X^\perp} invariant as well:
#{\langle Qy,x\rangle=\langle y,Qx\rangle=0}.
The resulting block diagonal matrix commutes with #{P}; conversely
#{PQ=QP} carries its image to itself.}
}

\p{When #{K\ne0}, the positive semidefinite difference
#{A-S_P} is nonzero, so the quadratic form decreases in some
direction. It need not be positive definite: some directions may
have zero loss. The assertion is a Loewner inequality with the
stated equality criterion, not a strict inequality in every direction.}

\p{For a positive-weight graph and a coordinate selector #{P_R},
#{K} consists of the entries of #{Q} between #{R} and its complement.
For distinct vertices these are minus the sum of weights of edges
joining them; orientation does not affect the sum. All summands are
positive. Hence #{K=0} exactly when no edge joins #{R} to its
complement. In a connected graph every nonempty proper coordinate
selector therefore induces a different precision. In particular,
the retained vertices in [cycle decimation](fdip-0057) are stable
under the retained cyclic action, but this symmetry does not make
their span #{Q}-invariant.}

\p{In orthonormal coordinates, Mathlib's
\leanref{Matrix.PosDef.inv} gives positivity of #{D^{-1}}.
The congruence lemma
\leanref{Matrix.PosSemidef.mul_mul_conjTranspose_same},
with its positive semidefinite matrix set to #{D^{-1}} and its
rectangular matrix set to #{K}, gives #{KD^{-1}K^*\succeq0}.
These declarations support the positivity steps. The equality
criterion, subspace equivalences and graph specialization are
proved above.}
